MidiMonitor2
(c)2026 David Beckman

I have adapted Bill Thibault's Midi Monitor 1.0 app, originally released for 
BeOS r4's MidiKit, to instead use the MidiKit2 of Haiku OS and BeOS r5.
While I was debugging my latest Haiku app, I found myself in need of one my
favorite MIDI tools for the BeOS, Midi Monitor.  The original binary and code 
was having issues running under Haiku_x86, so I fixed it, converted it to a 
MidiKit2 app, and did a little bit of other code cleanup.

From Bill's original description:
  MidiMonitor displays incoming MIDI messages.
  The user can select which physical MIDI input port to monitor.


  The color used to display a message is based on the
  "age" of the message: new messages in bright green,
  old messages in black. As messages age the color 
  fades to black: often just seeing the flash of
  an arriving message (from across the room, for example)
  is enough information to make the user happy.

  The window is fully resizable (based on William Adams' original
  Calendar example).  

  The code is not especially efficient, but will handle the
  densest MIDI streams without being swamped. The incoming
  MIDI stream is sampled at the "Pulse" rate (20 Hz).
  Only the most recent message is displayed.

R4 BeIDE projects for both PPC and Intel are still included.  
I know the original binaries worked in BeOS r5, but not sure 
if the project files will help with compiling for r5.  I will
find out soon, and update if necessary.

## Icon and Resources

The project includes resources compiled via Haiku's Resource Compiler (`rc`).
- [MidiMonitor.iom](MidiMonitor.iom): The source vector icon project file, editable in Icon-O-Matic.
- [MidiMonitor.rdef](MidiMonitor.rdef): The resource definition file.

To update/compile the vector icon:
1. Open `MidiMonitor.iom` in **Icon-O-Matic** on Haiku.
2. Go to **File > Export**, select **HVIF RDef** format, and save.
3. Open the exported text file, copy the hex string data block under `resource vector_icon`, and paste it into `MidiMonitor.rdef`.
4. Run `make` to compile the app. The Makefile will automatically compile the `.rdef` resource file and link it to the `MidiMonitor` binary.

## Installing

MidiMonitor is published on the Indispensable Labs apps repository. On Haiku:

```
pkgman add-repo https://hpkg.indispensablelabs.com/apps/x86_64/current
pkgman install midimonitor
```

x86_64 only for now. The sources are plain Be API C++98, so an x86_gcc2 build
should work, but none has been made yet -- there is no 32-bit repository.

Note that Haiku matches an existing repository by NAME, not by URL, so if the
repository ever moves you must `pkgman drop-repo "Indispensable Labs"` before
adding the new URL. `add-repo` alone prompts to overwrite and defaults to *no*.

## Packaging (.hpkg)

Everything lives in [packaging/](packaging/) and all of it must run on Haiku
except the last step. See the header comment in each script -- they carry the
reasoning, this is only the running order.

```
packaging/build-package.sh    # clean build + .hpkg into packaging/out/
packaging/build-repo.sh       # assemble the repository into packaging/out-repo/
packaging/publish-repo.sh     # dry run; add --go to actually upload
```

`make hpkg` is a shortcut for the first step alone.

`publish-repo.sh` runs where rclone is configured (WSL), not on Haiku, so copy
`out-repo/` off the Haiku box first.

**The repository is shared.** Beton is published to the same index, and
`package_repo create` writes a new index rather than amending one -- so
`build-repo.sh` downloads the live index, verifies every package it names
against the checksum recorded there, and re-indexes those alongside the local
build. Publishing MidiMonitor therefore republishes Beton unchanged. Two files
are duplicated verbatim in both repositories and must be kept byte-identical:
`packaging/repo-info.in` and `packaging/build-repo.sh`.

A HaikuPorts recipe (`midimonitor-2.0.1.recipe`) is included for a future
submission, but it is not what gets published and it does not build yet -- it
needs a release tag and a checksum first. Its header says so in detail.


